--- !u!114 &-5530733468383174069
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "Open the Lighting Window under <b>Window</b> > <b>Rendering</b>> <b>Lighting.</b>\n\nIn the Scene tab, notice that the Lighting Mode is set to <b>Baked Indirect,</b> to only capture the indirect lighting produced by the sky and the artificial lights placed in the scene. \n\nThe image above shows the exact same Scene with the Baked Lightmap debug view, showing the indirect light only.\n\nYou can learn more about <a href=\"https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/lighting-mode.html\">Lighting Modes</a> and their different use cases in the documentation."
